import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.29 Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.29 Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility","xkChars":0,"xkWords":564,"xkLessons":{"D.29":{"title":"D.29 Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility","lead":"City identifiers, geometrical areas, resident cohorts, and actual accessibility are different objects. Record neighborhood improvement separately from outcomes for original households and those who moved away.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5071–5086, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-29#section-d-29. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘After a map becomes connected, actual accessibility still depends on time periods, capacity, costs, admission, assistance needs, and alternative routes.’ The material conditions of urban formation, historical paths, and present agglomeration benefits differ; current benefits cannot provide a complete account of origins. Location, administrative jurisdiction, membership, functional networks, and service areas cannot share one boundary either. [T-main]","Using a common city list at each time point controls only differences between samples at that time. Only a list fixed across all time points yields a fixed statistical cohort. Even with a fixed cohort of identifiers, geometrical boundaries and residents can change. Fixed historical area and fixed current area can serve different indices; denominators must be disclosed. Fixing one element alone does not establish complete comparability. [T-main]","Specific methodological case: pages 34–35 of the UN's December 2025 WUP methodology report use 2025 city identifiers as the baseline and trace cities through overlaps. Nine centers in 2000 within Shanghai's 2025 boundary receive one ID, while Lagos and four centers connected in the 2050 projection retain separate identifiers. Continuity of identifiers is a tracking arrangement, not a claim of constant geometrical boundaries. [P-WUP]","‘No splitting or merging’ in the report's tracking rule does not mean actual urban spaces never split or merge. The 2050 figure is a projection, not completed history. The method seeks to improve comparison; it cannot instead establish that all analysis across time is invalid. The lesson author did not recalculate the grids and does not present a methodological description as independent data validation. [P-WUP]","Network centrality does not directly prove power or welfare. A facility's service area is a spatial representation with assumptions: continuous routes, service hours, bodily conditions, and independent use must be checked. Observe separately the visibility of a facility, lawful entry, affordable continuous access, safe activity, and the ability to change management rules. [T-main]","Everyday contact, staggered use, and local familiarity can support help-seeking and care, but safety, respect, and the ability to remain still require evidence. Local participation may omit tenants, future residents, and users from other districts; a majority of current residents cannot automatically speak for every affected position. [T-main]","Housing and land rights, building rights, tenancy security, mortgages, eligibility to return after redevelopment, and maintenance costs differ. The two specified pages of Roy 2005 criticize equating informality with poverty and note that lawful property title can coexist with violations of land-use rules. A settlement label is therefore insufficient to determine individual poverty or every legal status. The author's analysis of classificatory power does not authorize any particular disposition either. [T-main][P-ROY]","Statistical purposes can legitimately combine categories, but mappings and deduplication must be retained. Rural areas simultaneously support production, nonfarm work, care, and provision across places; they are not merely an earlier, backward urban stage. Roads may expand peripheral markets or intensify competition from central firms. Outcomes cannot be restricted in advance to positives or negatives. [T-main]","Slower relative growth is not absolute decline. The source's example of a redeveloped neighborhood with a higher mean but a different set of residents likewise requires retaining real changes in the current area without substituting them for living outcomes among original households or people who moved away. This does not reject the area indicator; it limits the object it explains. [T-main]","After a disaster, trace building safety and renewed exposure, household services, relationship networks, memory, feasible choices, and remedies separately. Relocation is not necessarily bad, and rebuilding in place is not necessarily good. A visible combination of partial recovery does not automatically prove a feedback trap; examine the looping mechanism further rather than judging by appearance. [T-main]","Connect to §§5.5.1–5.5.3 for aggregation, boundaries, and representation; Part X for relationships and circles; and §§15.3.7–15.3.8 for actual environmental uptake. The additional identification task is whether the object behind the same label has changed, whether improvement reaches the original people, and whether the path is genuinely usable. [T-main]","The eight entry points are: 29.01, different temporal questions about urban formation and agglomeration; 29.02, Lefebvre and Harvey on the production of space and rights; 29.03, Jacobs on local knowledge and diversity; 29.04, housing, land, rent, and segregation; 29.05, rural land institutions and urban–rural multifunctionality; 29.06, regional centers, peripheries, transport, and competition; 29.07, public space, accessibility, and everyday safety; and 29.08, post-disaster demolition, rebuilding, and memory. This explains research entry points without claiming a complete review of all the authors' original works. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Define separate administrative, geometrical, identifier, resident, and service-area boundaries.","Specify the fixed list, area denominator, and time span.","Measure continuous accessibility, not just connectivity on a map.","Separate rights concerning land, buildings, tenancy, return, and maintenance.","Track the area, original residents, those who left, renewed exposure, and remedies together."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Fixed identifier cohort","leftText":"The same set of IDs is tracked.","rightTitle":"Fixed geometry and residents","rightText":"The area and people may still change."},{"leftTitle":"A higher neighborhood mean","leftText":"A statistical change in the area as currently constituted.","rightTitle":"Improvement for original households","rightText":"Requires following the original people and those who moved away."},{"leftTitle":"An informality label","leftText":"A classification and description of processes.","rightTitle":"Specific legality","rightText":"Each right and action must be checked separately under the rules."},{"leftTitle":"Slower relative growth","leftText":"A lower growth rate than at other positions.","rightTitle":"Absolute decline","rightText":"A fall in its own quantity—a different proposition."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: redevelopment raises the mean while replacing the set of residents.","Source boundary: a visible combination of partial recovery does not automatically prove a feedback trap.","Teaching counterexample: a city keeps its ID while its area expands, changing the density denominator."],"questions":["Does the same identifier mean unchanged boundaries?","Can a label authorize demolition?","Does a higher mean mean improvement for original households?","Is a connected road enough to ensure access?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/figures/d29-boundary.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Tracking city identifiers across time in WUP2025","href":"https://population.un.org/wup/assets/Publications/undesa_pd_2025_wup2025_methodological_report.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Tracking city identifiers across time in WUP2025","text":"Pages 34–35 explain that nine historical centers in Shanghai share one ID, while centers connected to Lagos in the 2050 projection retain separate identifiers, preserving tracking relationships.","sourceLabel":"UN DESA: World Urbanization Prospects 2025 Methodology","sourceUrl":"https://population.un.org/wup/assets/Publications/undesa_pd_2025_wup2025_methodological_report.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed pages 34–35 (PDF pages 44–45), the MTUC entry and Shanghai/Lagos examples","boundary":"The method does not guarantee constant geometry, and 2050 is a projection. The grids were not recalculated, nor was the full report reviewed.","href":"https://population.un.org/wup/assets/Publications/undesa_pd_2025_wup2025_methodological_report.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.29","start_line":5162,"end_line":5177,"startLine":5162,"endLine":5177}],"outputId":"section-d-29","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a higher mean through replacement of residents","text":"In a fictional neighborhood, two original households have incomes of 10 and 20, averaging 15. Both later leave, and two new households have incomes of 30 and 40, averaging 35. The current area's mean genuinely rises by 20, while the original households' incomes after departure remain unknown.","assumptions":"Artificial complete replacement of residents, a consistent measure, and no causal explanation.","boundary":"This does not imply all redevelopment involves replacement or all relocation is bad."},{"title":"Teaching construction: relative lag with absolute growth","text":"Artificial peripheral output rises from 100 to 110, while central output rises from 100 to 130. The periphery grows 10% and the center 30%. The periphery grows more slowly in relative terms while increasing by 10 in absolute terms. With central output as denominator, the ratio falls from 1 to 11/13, but this cannot be described as absolute peripheral contraction.","assumptions":"Fictional output in common units, not welfare or net benefits.","boundary":"This does not prove that roads caused the changes."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: redevelopment raises the mean while replacing the set of residents.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: a visible combination of partial recovery does not automatically prove a feedback trap.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a city keeps its ID while its area expands, changing the density denominator.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does the same identifier mean unchanged boundaries?","answer":"No. Examine the tracking and spatial rules.","basis":"The real methodological case."},{"question":"Can a label authorize demolition?","answer":"No. Specific rights and procedures require separate checks.","basis":"Authority."},{"question":"Does a higher mean mean improvement for original households?","answer":"No. The people may have changed; follow the original people.","basis":"Cohorts."},{"question":"Is a connected road enough to ensure access?","answer":"Time, capacity, costs, admission, and assistance conditions are also needed.","basis":"Accessibility."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/figures/d29-boundary.png","alt":"Fixed IDs do not fix geometry or residents; the UN's tracking and separate-identifier rules differ.","caption":"The methodological limits of WUP2025."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/figures/d29-residents.png","alt":"Artificial original mean 15, current mean 35; subsequent outcomes for the original people are unknown.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/figures/d29-access.png","alt":"Moving from visible connectivity to independent, safe use requires specific conditions along the path.","caption":"Based on D.29."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures distinguish urban tracking, resident composition, and actual accessibility.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["Actual household outcomes after departure and the urban data grids were not checked.","The empirical studies cited by Roy were not individually reviewed; they do not establish eligibility for a particular disposition."],"taskId":"S-D-29","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.29, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4983–4998","claim_support":["Multiple spatial boundaries, accessibility, housing rights, rural areas, redevelopment and disaster aftermath, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-WUP","title":"UN DESA: World Urbanization Prospects 2025 Methodology","url":"https://population.un.org/wup/assets/Publications/undesa_pd_2025_wup2025_methodological_report.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Methodology report, December 2025","locator":"Printed pages 34–35 (PDF pages 44–45), the MTUC entry and Shanghai/Lagos examples","claim_support":["Tracking uses 2025 identifiers: nine historical centers in Shanghai receive the same ID, while centers connected in the 2050 Lagos model keep separate identifiers. An identifier does not mean a fixed geometrical boundary."],"limits":["The lesson author directly read the MTUC text on the two specified pages, without claiming to read all 60 pages or recalculate the grids. The 2050 example is a projection scenario, not an event that has already occurred."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-ROY","title":"Ananya Roy: Urban Informality: Toward an Epistemology of Planning","url":"https://www.wiego.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/Urban-Informality-Roy.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005，JAPA71(2)","locator":"2005, printed pages 148–149 (PDF pages 2–3)","claim_support":["The source criticizes equating informality with poverty, explains that lawful title can coexist with violations of land-use rules, and discusses the state's classificatory power."],"limits":["The lesson author read the two specified pages in full, without checking every empirical study cited throughout the article. This is the author's theoretical argument, not a ruling on a household's rights, and it creates no power to evict or otherwise dispose of a case."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.29 Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility; Chinese source lines 5071–5086; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/source/S-D-29.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-29/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"884977ff866de83547e0e875552e0bd3a054292137394f4ae8ed3d3d6107a8f8","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-29.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-29.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-29.md"};function d(h,e,u,p,m,g){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-29" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.29</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-29" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.29 Cities, Space, Housing, Rural Areas, and Public Accessibility {#section-d-29}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §5.5.1–5.5.3, §10, and §15.3.7–15.3.8. Research entries cover 29.01–29.08.</p><p>Distinguish the material conditions for urban formation, historical paths, and current agglomeration benefits. Geographic location, administrative jurisdiction, membership, functional networks, and service areas also have distinct boundaries. When the same city ID is used across periods, check the statistical coverage and tracking rules. An unchanged ID does not establish a fixed spatial boundary (United Nations Population Division, 2025). Once connectivity is established on a map, actual access still depends on times of access, capacity, cost, eligibility, support needs, and alternative routes. Centrality does not directly establish power or welfare.</p><p>Everyday contact, use at staggered times, and familiarity with a place can support help-seeking and looking out for others. Safety, basic respect, and users’ ability to remain in place still require evidence. Local participation can also exclude renters, future residents, and users from other areas. Observe separately whether a space is visible, whether it can be used lawfully, whether affordable access is continuous, whether people can use it safely and autonomously, and whether people can change the rules under which it is managed.</p><p>Distinguish land rights, building rights, tenure security for renters, mortgages, eligibility to return, and maintenance costs. A neighborhood’s name and appearance do not by themselves establish an individual’s poverty, a violation of law, or eligibility for intervention (see Roy, 2005). When combining categories for a specific statistical use, retain the classification mapping and deduplicate the records. Rural areas support production, nonfarm work, care, and supply across places. They are not a less advanced stage of urban development. New roads can expand peripheral markets and can also intensify competition as firms from the center enter. Growing more slowly in relative terms does not amount to absolute decline.</p><p>Teaching example. A redeveloped neighborhood has a higher average income, but a different set of residents. The change in the current neighborhood can be real. It cannot stand in for outcomes in the lives of original households or those who moved away. After a disaster, track building safety and renewed exposure, services for households, relationship networks, local memory, feasible choices, and remedies separately. Moving is not necessarily bad, and rebuilding in place is not necessarily good. The observable pattern of partial recovery does not automatically establish a feedback trap.</p><p>Using a common list of cities at each time point addresses differences in the sample at that time only. A fixed statistical cohort requires holding the list fixed across all time points. Even a fixed cohort does not ensure that geometric boundaries or residents stay the same. Holding the historical area fixed and holding the current area fixed can serve different valid indexes. State the denominator explicitly. A facility’s service area is a spatial representation based on assumptions. Continuous routes, actual times of access, people’s physical circumstances, and autonomous use still require corresponding evidence.</p><p>Research topics. 29.01 Urban formation and agglomeration. 29.02 The production of space and the right to the city (Lefebvre and Harvey). 29.03 Local knowledge and urban diversity (Jacobs and others). 29.04 Housing, land, ground rent, and differentiation. 29.05 Rural society, land institutions, and urban–rural relations. 29.06 Regional development, centers and peripheries, and transportation networks. 29.07 Public space, accessibility, and everyday safety. 29.08 Postdisaster reconstruction, demolition and relocation, and local memory.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.29"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
